 before i  bring in daniel from moscow without  russia's presence then it will be  difficult to address these economic  problems  will it not be  yes the main theme of g20 is recovered  together we cover stronger and we  definitely highlight the inclusiveness  and togetherness to this poor economic  growth at the post panamic time so  without russia it's definitely  you know it's hindered the potential of  the inclusive economic good i understand  the political crisis will give a  different story but that's definitely  something that we don't want to see in  g20 2020 by indonesia all right daniel  um you were mentioning this a moment ago  and you were talking to us about china's  position of course it hasn't condemned  the invasion of ukraine it's also  criticized western sanctions and  defended moscow as an ally uh it's  called russia an important member of the  g20 as well now the chances of one of  the g20 members like china vetoing  russia is  pretty high isn't it  well actually yes absolutely i think  that of course the majority of the g20  members they are comprised of the  countries which are currently are not  extremely  favoring what's going on between russia  and ukraine of course but still i think  that  if we again recall the example of g8 so  in order to expel russia there should be  a unanimous decision by all the members  like it happened when g7 countries  bittered russian participation and  russia back at 2013 russia was not  expelled as it is it was not just  invited to the other meetings and so  russia had no other choice but just to  withdraw its kind of  goodwill participation in such kind of  summits and so it it's not likely to  happen now because at least we have  china which has already expressed its  absolute disagreement with the idea of  return russia participation and we also  have some other states and i think that  here we can follow what's going to  happen for example from the side of  india from the site of brazil or for  example from the site of south africa  because as far as we know for example  india and south africa did not  vote in favor of the recent u.n general  assembly resolution criticized in  russian events in ukraine and so they  abstain and i think that if we speak  about upcoming g20 event uh we could  also closely monitor how greeks  countries are going to react because  as as far as we know china has already  sided with russia on g20 issue and so  what's going to happen with india south  africa and brazil it's still the matter  of time and we'll see but even with  china being against the idea of  excluding russia i think that this  initiative is not going to be  materialized in this particular sense  okay elizabeth go ahead  i mean i'd just like to say this is not  the u.n security council neither china  nor russia has a veto here it's not it  you know there's nothing laid out as  we've heard that indicates exactly how a  country can be booted from the g20 this  is a matter of invitation and if you've  got it at my account you've got at least  half of the g20 countries that i believe  are going to strongly oppose russia's  participation in the summit the  invitation is going to come from the  current g20 president which is indonesia  so it's going to be very easy for  indonesia to make that determination but  at the same time we're not talking about  a situation where there's something laid  out in the regulations here where the  g20 has to unanimously decide on  anything that they do this is it's not  an organization that works that way and  so you don't have to get china on board  if at least half of the g20 countries  oppose it and even if the decision is to  move forward and allow russia to come  then uh the boycott by a large  percentage of the countries of the g20  is going to call into question what this  organization is really good for which  brings me to my next question to you in  fact elizabeth i mean how likely is it  that other leaders will not come if the  russian president shows up  i mean i would find it very hard to  believe that you know this is months  away so we're not really sure what the  situation specifically will be but if  we're in a similar situation to what we  are and now i find it hard to believe  that the united states european  countries and other countries that have  expressed uh condemnation of this  invasion would find it easy enough to  look the other way and act as though its  business as usual with russia at this  stage uh many countries in the world are  using all of the all of the messaging  all of the tools that they have to try  and and press for a resolution to this  war and for russia to take back its  aggression aggressive stance but um  going about and acting as though  business is normal particularly when you  have massive sanctions even if this is  an economic forum  you simply can't act as though there are  other  other global crises that russia can be a  positive player in at this time so i  think it's going to be highly unlikely  to see everyone at that table  what do you think the u.s will do in  particular and is this something we see  the president president biden right now  in in brussels obviously meeting with uh  nato is this something that he's going  to be pushing for during his meetings  this week to to suspend russia or expel  russia  i mean i i don't think that's going to  be the number one priority but because  there are more urgent issues at um at  the fore right now but i do believe it's  going to be a matter of discussion and  of course with putin making the  announcement about the intention to join  the g20 summit that is put it on the  table and in the news but i imagine it's  going to be a discussion of the many  different ways that the the alliances  against russia's actions in ukraine can  make their position known and make it  harder for russia to continue to act as  though things are normal the g20 is one  of many multilateral institutions that  functions based on you know  international norms generally being  respected that's how our global economy  works so if you have one country in that  set that is flagrantly violating those  uh those norms and those rules you  cannot go to another forum that's  related to that same  global situation to that same  international  world that we all function in and just  pretend like the violations in one part  of that scenario aren't going to affect  every other one and i believe they're  going to be talking about that today and  they're going to be talking about every  form in which this issue can be raised  daniel do you agree with what elizabeth  has to say  yeah actually i would like to make one  comment to the to the elizabeth opinion  about  g20  being not an organization of course it's  not an organization but what i meant is  that it's not going to be in the way of  voting that half of the members of  organization vote against participation  of russia and so that's uh that's it  that's how it works i think that  in order to absolutely ban russia out  indonesia can only for example uh  suspend issue visas to the diplomats of  uh russia and the participants of  russian delegation but i don't see any  other possible way a way to prevent  russia from going to this event even  though uh the countries uh or all of the  half of the g20 members say that no  we're against russia but it's not going  to be a vote and it's not going to be in  this  manner and that's why actually today we  also  would see a lot of reports saying that  indonesia uh has come under  some pressure from the western countries  saying that  it should think again about whether  russia should be invited or not but  russian side has expressed its  willingness to come to the event and the  russian president  has been said that he was going to  participate at least for today we don't  know how this situation is going to  change and so just to ban russia from  the participation in this forum i think  the only possible way is 